= Generalization (machine learning) — Technical =

== Expected risk ==
For hypothesis h and target distribution P:

<pre>
R(h) = E_(x,y)~P [ℓ(h(x),y)]
</pre>

Given training sample S:

<pre>
R_S(h) = (1/n) Σ_i ℓ(h(x_i),y_i)
</pre>

Generalization analysis studies how R(h) relates to R_S(h) for h chosen using S.

== Uniform and algorithm-dependent approaches ==
Classical approaches bound generalization through complexity measures such as VC dimension, Rademacher complexity, margins, stability, or compression.

Modern deep-learning theory also studies optimization bias, implicit regularization, interpolation, scale, and data structure.

== Shift ==
If training distribution P_train differs from target P_target, ordinary iid generalization bounds may no longer describe deployment risk.

This motivates domain adaptation, robustness, invariant prediction, and distribution-shift analysis.

